WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:05.050
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:05.050 --> 00:00:10.720
Today we are going to play with a cellular
automaton and fuse it together with a neural

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:11.730
network.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.559
It will be quite an experience!

00:00:13.559 --> 00:00:18.030
But of course, first, what are these things
anyway?

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:24.050
You can imagine a cellular automaton as a
small game where we have a bunch of cells,

00:00:24.050 --> 00:00:29.600
and a set of simple rules that describe when
a cell should be full, and when it should

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:30.920
be empty.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.140
What you see here is a popular example called
Game of Life, which simulates a tiny world

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:39.460
where each cell represents a little life form.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.760
So why is this so interesting?

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:48.390
Well, this cellular automaton shows us that
even a small set of simple rules can give

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:55.320
rise to remarkably complex life forms, such
as gliders, spaceships, and even John von

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.980
Neumann’s universal constructor, or in other
words, self-replicating machines.

00:01:00.980 --> 00:01:08.000
Now, this gets more interesting, a later paper
fused this cellular automaton with a neural

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.160
network.

00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:15.270
It was tasked to grow, and even better, maintain
a prescribed shape.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:19.280
Remember these two words, grow and maintain
shape.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:25.200
And the question was, if it can recover from
undesirable states, can it perhaps..regenerate

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:26.310
when damaged?

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:33.520
Well, here, you will see all kinds of damage…and
then, this happens.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:34.520
Nice!

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:39.260
The best part is that this thing wasn’t
even trained to be able to perform this kind

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:40.740
of regeneration!

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:45.980
The objective for training was that it should
be able to perform its task of growing and

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:52.140
maintaining shape, and it turns out, some
sort of regeneration is included in that.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:58.260
This sounds very promising, and I wonder if
we can we apply this concept to something

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.930
where healing is instrumental?

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.830
Are there such applications in computer science?

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:06.220
If so, what could those be?

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:09.149
Oh yes, yes there are!

00:02:09.149 --> 00:02:12.720
For instance, think about texture synthesis.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:17.171
This is a topic that is subject to a great
deal of research in computer graphics, and

00:02:17.171 --> 00:02:20.480
those folks have this down to a science.

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:22.849
So, what are we doing here?

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:28.819
Texture synthesis typically means that we
need lots of concrete or gravel road, skin,

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:35.439
marble, create unique stripes for zebras,
for instance, for a computer game or the post-production

00:02:35.439 --> 00:02:41.060
of a movie, and we really don’t want to
draw miles and miles of these textures by

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:42.060
hand.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:46.700
Instead, we give it to an algorithm to continue
this small sample, where the output should

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:51.469
be a bigger version of this pattern, with
the same characteristics.

00:02:51.469 --> 00:02:55.499
So how do we know if we have a good technique
at hand?

00:02:55.499 --> 00:03:02.700
Well, first, it must not be repetitive, checkmark,
and it has to be free of seams.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:07.750
This part means that we should not be able
to see any lines or artifacts that would quickly

00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:09.420
give the trick away.

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:17.560
Now, get this, this new paper attempts to
do the same with neural cellular automatons.

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:19.560
What an insane idea!

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:23.549
We like those around here, so let’s give
it a try!

00:03:23.549 --> 00:03:24.549
How?

00:03:24.549 --> 00:03:29.450
Well, first, by trying to expand this simple
checkerboard pattern.

00:03:29.450 --> 00:03:36.140
The algorithm is starting out from random
noise, and as it evolves…well, this is a

00:03:36.140 --> 00:03:37.140
disaster.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.810
We are looking for squares, but we have quadrilaterals.

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:43.520
They are also misaligned.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.239
And they are also inconsistent.

00:03:45.239 --> 00:03:48.669
But, luckily, we are not done yet.

00:03:48.669 --> 00:03:54.290
And now, hold on to your papers, and observe
how the grid cells communicate with each other

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:55.980
to improve the result.

00:03:55.980 --> 00:04:02.670
First, the misalignment is taken care of,
then, the quadrilaterals become squares, and

00:04:02.670 --> 00:04:06.389
then, the consistency of their placement is
improved.

00:04:06.389 --> 00:04:09.749
And the end result is, look!

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:15.340
In this other example, we can not only see
these beautiful bubbles grow out of nowhere.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:21.220
But the density of the bubbles remains roughly
the same over the process.

00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.220
Look!

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:27.470
As two of them get too close to each other,
they coalesce, or pop.

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:29.980
Damaged bubbles can also regrow.

00:04:29.980 --> 00:04:32.060
Very cool!

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:38.169
Okay, it can do proper texture synthesis,
but so can a ton of other handcrafted computer

00:04:38.169 --> 00:04:42.430
graphics algorithms, so why is this interesting?

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:43.880
Why bother with this?

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:49.889
Well, first, you may think that the result
of this technique is the same as other techniques,

00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:51.640
but it isn’t.

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:57.229
The output is not necessarily just an image,
but can be an animation too!

00:04:57.229 --> 00:04:58.229
Excellent.

00:04:58.229 --> 00:05:04.900
Here, it was also able to animate the checkerboard
pattern, and, even better, it can not only

00:05:04.900 --> 00:05:10.780
reproduce the weave pattern, but the animation
part extends to this too.

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.280
And now comes the even more interesting part.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.729
Let’s ask why does it output an animation
and not an image?

00:05:18.729 --> 00:05:21.810
The answer lies within these weaving patterns.

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:24.569
We just need to carefully observe them.

00:05:24.569 --> 00:05:25.940
Let’s see.

00:05:25.940 --> 00:05:32.860
Yes, again, we start out from noise, where
some woven patterns emerge, but then, it almost

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:38.379
looks like a person who started weaving them
until it resembles the initial sample.

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:40.669
Yes, that is the key!

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:46.539
The neural network learned to create not an
image, not an animation, but no less than

00:05:46.539 --> 00:05:51.280
a computer program to accomplish this kind
of texture synthesis!

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:53.169
How cool is that?

00:05:53.169 --> 00:05:59.380
So, armed with all that knowledge, do you
remember the regenerating iguana project?

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:05.389
Let’s try to destroy these textures too
and see if it can use these computer programs

00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:09.500
to recover and get us a seamless texture.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:16.830
First, we delete parts of the texture, then,
it fills in the gap with noise, and now, let’s

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.180
run that program!

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:21.289
Wow!

00:06:21.289 --> 00:06:25.909
Resilient, self-healing texture synthesis.

00:06:25.909 --> 00:06:27.909
How cool is that?

00:06:27.909 --> 00:06:34.110
And in every case, it starts out from a solution
that is completely wrong, improves it to be

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:40.250
just kind of wrong, and after further improvement,
there you go.

00:06:40.250 --> 00:06:41.350
Fantastic!

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:42.850
What a time to be alive!

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:48.979
And, note that this is a paper in the wonderful
Distill journal, which not only means that

00:06:48.979 --> 00:06:56.129
it is excellent, but also interactive, so
you can run many of these experiments yourself

00:06:56.129 --> 00:06:57.740
right in your web browser.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:46.139
The link is available 

00:07:46.139 --> 00:07:55.810
in the video description.

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:59.429
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

